{"title":"Paloma Streaming","sub":"Should a niche streaming service raise its price?","type":"Pricing","industry":"Digital media","geo":"Spain","diff":1,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Price elasticity · break-even volume loss","prompt":"Paloma is a Spanish streaming service for independent and classic cinema with 180,000 subscribers paying $6.99 a month. Content costs are rising and the CEO wants to raise the price to $8.49. She asks whether it's a good idea.","clarify":[{"q":"Cost per subscriber?","a":"About $1.50 a month: streaming, payment fees and per-view royalties."},{"q":"What happened last time prices rose?","a":"Paloma raised the price two years ago, by a similar proportion (about 20%). The interviewer has the subscriber data."},{"q":"Competitors?","a":"The big streaming platforms cost more but have little classic or independent film."},{"q":"Objective?","a":"Higher monthly profit, without damaging the brand."}],"structure":{"root":"Does the price rise increase profit?","branches":[{"head":"Margin per subscriber","items":["New price minus cost per subscriber"]},{"head":"Subscribers","items":["How many cancel because of the rise","Engaged vs casual viewers"]},{"head":"Longer-term effects","items":["Fewer new sign-ups","Brand and word of mouth"]}]},"hypothesis":"Paloma's subscribers are film enthusiasts with few alternatives, so I expect low price sensitivity. The rise likely pays off unless more than about a fifth of subscribers leave.","questions":[{"q":"Here is what happened to subscribers after the last price rise, announced in March. What do you take from it?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"398.531281pt\" height=\"167.269pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 398.531281 167.269\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 167.269 L 398.531281